# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PRODUCTION — loaded by `npm run build`. See .env.example for the full reference
# on every variable; this file records only the values and the decisions behind
# the ones that were open.
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE IS NOW COMMITTED, WHICH IT WAS NOT BEFORE 2026-09-13.
#   `.gitignore` matched `.env*` with only `.env.example` excepted, so this file
#   existed on one laptop and nowhere else — and CI has no other way to learn the
#   production hosts. Everything in it is `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, which Next inlines into
#   the JavaScript bundle sent to every visitor, so none of it is secret and none
#   of it can become secret. Nothing without that prefix belongs here.
#
# ⚠ THE VALUES ARE BAKED IN AT BUILD TIME. Editing this file does nothing to a
#   running container; it takes a new image. That is why the image is built per
#   branch and why `production` is the only branch that publishes one.
#
# ⚠ `.env.local` STILL OVERRIDES THIS FILE, AND THAT MATTERS LOCALLY.
#   A developer's `.env.local` points at localhost:8022 and beats everything
#   here, which is correct for `npm run dev`. It is excluded from the Docker build
#   context (see .dockerignore) precisely so it can never win inside an image —
#   if it could, a production build would silently ship a localhost API.
#
#   Now that the production hosts are live, the two overrides in `.env.local` are
#   no longer needed to make `npm run build` work on a workstation, and that file
#   says as much. Deleting them is safe; leaving them only affects local builds.
#
# ⚠ Note the hyphen: `wimall.com` belongs to another company and must never
#   appear anywhere.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─── Origins ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# jovi-mall, the commerce API. Verified live on 2026-09-13: GET /api/health
# answers 200, and a CORS preflight carrying Origin: https://wi-mall.com comes
# back with a matching Access-Control-Allow-Origin and
# Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true, so this origin is in the service's
# exact-match allowlist.
#
# ⚠ NO /api SUFFIX HERE, unlike the two dashboards. This app's client appends the
#   path itself; the dashboards' `VITE_API_BASE_URL` includes it. The two
#   conventions differ and both are correct for their own repository.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.wi-mall.com

# The canonical origin. This is not cosmetic: it is the base for every canonical
# link, the sitemap, robots.txt, the OpenGraph URLs, and `openExternal()` in the
# packaged app. A wrong value points the whole SEO surface at the wrong host, and
# search engines act on it long before anyone notices.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://wi-mall.com

# ─── Live delivery tracking (geo-tracker, the second service) ─────────────────
# SET 2026-09-13 — geo-tracker is deployed and this is its real origin. Verified
# the same day: GET https://track.wi-mall.com/readyz answers 200, and a preflight
# carrying Origin: https://wi-mall.com is allowed, so this origin is in
# geo-tracker's ALLOWED_ORIGINS — the single setting that governs both its HTTP
# CORS and its WebSocket origin check.
#
# Unversioned and mounted at the root, so there is no /api prefix. The socket URL
# is derived from this by swapping the scheme, so an https origin yields wss
# automatically and there is nothing else to configure.
#
# ⚠ IT WAS UNSET BEFORE, AND UNSET MEANS NO TRACKING PANEL AT ALL — not a
#   degraded one. For a delivery marketplace that is a real feature missing, which
#   is why it is switched on now that the service exists.
#
# ✅ THE BACKEND SETTING THIS RELIES ON IS CONFIRMED PRESENT. A browser
#   authenticates the tracking socket with the httpOnly `access_token` cookie,
#   which only reaches track.wi-mall.com if that cookie is scoped to the parent
#   domain. jovi-mall sets it with domain = AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN in production
#   (src/config/cookie.config.ts), and the deployed environment has
#   AUTH_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.wi-mall.com. Checked 2026-09-13.
#
#   ⚠ KEPT BECAUSE IT IS STILL THE FIRST THING TO CHECK IF TRACKING EVER BREAKS.
#   Were it emptied, the cookie becomes host-only to api.wi-mall.com and never
#   reaches geo-tracker — and the symptom misleads: every ordinary page and API
#   call keeps working, because those go to the host that owns the cookie, and ONLY
#   live tracking fails, as a socket that opens and is then closed unauthenticated.
#   It looks like a geo-tracker fault rather than a cookie-scope one.
#
#   ⚠ Still verify with a real order on a real phone, not from the execution list.
#   The cookie scope being right does not prove the whole path works.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GEO_TRACKER_URL=https://track.wi-mall.com

# ─── Map tiles ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SET 2026-09-13 — LocationIQ raster tiles. A map is a grid of small square images
# and this is the pattern the map code fills in per square: {z} is the zoom, {x}/{y}
# are the square's position, {s} is a subdomain (a/b/c) that lets the browser fetch
# several at once instead of queueing on one hostname.
#
# ⚠ LOCATIONIQ RATHER THAN GEOAPIFY, AND THE REASON IS THE KEY, NOT THE MAP.
#   Both providers were tested live on 2026-09-13 with the real keys:
#
#     LocationIQ  200 from wi-mall.com and agency.wi-mall.com, 403 from
#                 example.com and 403 with no referrer at all.
#     Geoapify    200 from example.com, and 200 with no referrer.
#
#   So the LocationIQ key is correctly restricted to these two domains and the
#   Geoapify one is not restricted at all. A tile key is PUBLIC by construction —
#   it ships inside the JavaScript every visitor downloads — so the domain
#   restriction is the only thing standing between it and anyone else's map.
#   Geoapify's worked everywhere, which is exactly the problem.
#
#   ⚠ It first looked the other way round: LocationIQ 403'd every test until the
#     requests carried a Referer. The refusal WAS the restriction working.
#
# ⚠ Verified against the live service, not read off a page: streets/dark/light all
#   return real PNGs, subdomains a/b/c all answer, and z=20 is the deepest zoom —
#   z=21 is a 400. Neither key is the one the backend uses for routing (checked by
#   hash), so there is no shared quota between the map and checkout's geocoding.
#
# ⚠ NO {r} IN THE TEMPLATE, DELIBERATELY. This app turns Leaflet's detectRetina on
#   only when the URL contains {r}; LocationIQ does not document a retina
#   placeholder, so leaving it out is what stops Leaflet requesting addresses that
#   would 404.
#
# ⚠ THE ATTRIBUTION IS A LICENCE CONDITION, NOT DECORATION. The tiles are
#   OpenStreetMap-derived and free on the condition that the credit is shown.
#   ⚠ LocationIQ's own docs page does not state the exact required wording, so this
#     credits both LocationIQ and OpenStreetMap — the conservative reading. Worth
#     confirming against their terms of service once.
#
# ⚠ TILES DO NOT FAIL OVER, UNLIKE ROUTING. geo-tracker picks a routing provider
#   from a chain, but it serves no tiles at all (checked: its provider chain exposes
#   GetRoute and GetDistanceMatrix, no tile endpoint), and the browser fetches tiles
#   straight from LocationIQ. If they have an outage the BACKGROUND goes blank while
#   the route line, the courier marker, the distance and the ETA all keep working,
#   because those come from the backend. A tile outage makes the map plain; it does
#   not break tracking.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL=https://{s}-tiles.locationiq.com/v3/streets/r/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=pk.ce28c81ac49b438476b3d7e53c08fc08
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL_DARK=https://{s}-tiles.locationiq.com/v3/dark/r/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=pk.ce28c81ac49b438476b3d7e53c08fc08
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION=&copy; <a href="https://locationiq.com/">LocationIQ</a> &copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors
# 20 is LocationIQ's real ceiling, measured. The code default is 19, so stating
# it buys one extra zoom level of street detail for a customer following a courier.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_MAX_ZOOM=20

# ─── Sign-in entry points ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Customers have NO PASSWORD. They sign in through the WhatsApp or Telegram bot,
# and these two values are the whole way in — if they are wrong the site looks
# perfect and nobody can sign in, silently.
#
# ⚠ BOTH CONFIRMED BY THE PROJECT OWNER ON 2026-09-13.
#   This file previously carried a warning that `+15557845447` was a Meta SANDBOX
#   number and `kalynux_bot` a personal test bot, and that both had to be replaced
#   before launch. Half of that was wrong and half was right:
#
#     - the NUMBER is the real production bot number and stays as it is;
#     - the BOT NAME has changed. It is now `WiMallBot`, not `kalynux_bot`.
#       Verified the same day: https://t.me/WiMallBot resolves to a bot titled
#       "Wi-Mall".
#
# ⚠ THE BOT NAME MUST MATCH jovi-mall's `TELEGRAM_BOT_NAME` AND THE TOKEN n8n
#   HOLDS. Three places name the same bot and nothing compares them: this value
#   only builds the link a customer clicks, so pointing it at a bot that nothing
#   is listening on produces a chat that is simply never answered. There is no
#   error anywhere in that path.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_NUMBER=+15557845447
NEXT_PUBLIC_TELEGRAM_BOT_NAME=WiMallBot

# ─── Payments ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DELIBERATELY EMPTY as of 2026-09-13. It carried a pk_test_ key, which did
# nothing and implied more than was true.
#
# Stripe is switched OFF on the backend: STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is absent from the
# deployed jovi-mall environment, and its absence IS the off switch. NotchPay and
# MyCoolPay carry real payments. A publishable key with no secret key behind it
# cannot complete a charge, so shipping one only makes the dead path look live.
#
# ⚠ SETTING THIS WOULD NOT SWITCH CARD PAYMENTS ON, AND CLEARING IT DOES NOT
#   SWITCH THEM OFF. Checkout's option list is unconditional — verified in
#   src/components/shop/PaymentMethodPicker.tsx, where CHECKOUT_OPTIONS is
#   [MTN, ORANGE, CARD, COD] and the checkout page filters it only for
#   COD-on-digital. So the "Card (Visa or Mastercard)" option renders either way,
#   and `initiatePayment` returns a clientSecret that no frontend has ever
#   consumed. That is a pre-existing product gap, not a deployment setting, and it
#   is the one thing on this page a customer can reach that cannot succeed.
#   Removing the option or building the flow is a decision for the shop, not for
#   this file.
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ─── Agent app store listings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Empty until published: the download buttons then render DISABLED with a
# "Coming soon" pill rather than as dead links. The agent app is Android/iOS only
# and ships through the stores, so there is no agent.wi-mall.com to point at and
# these stay empty until those listings exist.
NEXT_PUBLIC_AGENT_APP_ANDROID_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_AGENT_APP_IOS_URL=
